::STAT_U01_L02_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records only each student's commute time in minutes. What structure is this?
{
=A. Univariate, because one variable is recorded#Commute time is the only variable for each student.
~B. Bivariate, because many students are included#Many cases do not create two variables.
~C. Bivariate, because time uses numbers#Numerical data can still be univariate.
~D. Neither, because minutes are not categories#The issue is number of variables, not whether the variable is categorical.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records study time and quiz score for each student. Which display best explores the relationship?
{
=A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot is appropriate for two numerical variables.
~B. Bar chart#A bar chart compares category counts.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a whole for one categorical variable.
~D. Dot plot of quiz scores only#A one-variable dot plot would not show the relationship with study time.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A table compares grade level and preferred homework location. What variables are recorded?
{
~A. One numerical variable#There are two variables, and neither is a measured quantity.
~B. Two numerical variables#The variables are labels or groups, not measurements.
~C. One categorical and one numerical variable#Both variables describe groups.
=D. Two categorical variables#Grade level and preferred location are both categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which situation is bivariate?
{
~A. Recording only favorite color#That is one categorical variable.
~B. Recording only test score#That is one numerical variable.
~C. Counting how many students responded#A count of responses is not two variables for each case.
=D. Recording height and arm span for each student#Two measurements are recorded for each student.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best fits two categorical variables?
{
~A. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show two numerical variables.
~B. Histogram#A histogram shows one numerical variable.
=C. Two-way frequency table#A two-way table summarizes counts for combinations of two categories.
~D. Dot plot#A dot plot usually shows one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A scatterplot shows hours practiced and number of free throws made. What can it help describe?
{
=A. The relationship between two numerical variables#Scatterplots show patterns such as positive, negative, or no association.
~B. The percent of students in each category#That is categorical relative frequency.
~C. The exact cause of better performance#A scatterplot can suggest association but does not prove cause.
~D. The count in each sport category#The variables are numerical measurements, not categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A question asks, "Which snack is chosen most often?" What type of display is usually appropriate?
{
~A. Scatterplot#There are not two numerical variables.
~B. Histogram#Snack names are categories, not numerical intervals.
=C. Bar chart#The variable is categorical, so category counts can be compared with bars.
~D. Line of best fit#A line of best fit is used with scatterplots for numerical relationships.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A student calls a data set bivariate because it has 30 students. What is the misconception?
{
~A. Bivariate means exactly 30 cases#Case count does not define bivariate data.
~B. Bivariate means all values are numerical#Bivariate data may include categorical or numerical variables.
=C. Bivariate means two variables, not many cases#The number of cases is different from the number of variables.
~D. Bivariate means the data are displayed in a chart#Display format does not define the number of variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which data set is univariate categorical?
{
~A. Height and weight for each student#That is bivariate numerical.
~B. Study time and test score#That is bivariate numerical.
~C. Number of books read for each student#That is univariate numerical, not categorical.
=D. Favorite type of movie for each student#One categorical variable is recorded for each student.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which data set is bivariate numerical?
{
~A. Favorite flavor only#That is one categorical variable.
=B. Temperature and ice cream sales for each day#Two numerical variables are recorded for each day.
~C. Grade level and lunch choice#That is two categorical variables.
~D. Daily rainfall only#That is one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A segmented bar graph compares preferred study method by grade level. What does it help interpret?
{
~A. The relationship between two numerical measurements#That would usually use a scatterplot.
~B. The shape of one numerical distribution#That would use a dot plot or histogram.
~C. The exact value for every individual student#The graph summarizes groups, not individual records.
=D. Relative frequencies across categories#Segmented bars compare category proportions between groups.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records age and favorite app category. How should it be described?
{
~A. Univariate numerical#There are two variables, not one.
=B. Bivariate with one numerical and one categorical variable#Age is numerical and app category is categorical.
~C. Bivariate with two numerical variables#Favorite app category is categorical.
~D. Univariate categorical#Age is an additional numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best fits one numerical variable such as points scored per game?
{
~A. Two-way table#A two-way table summarizes two categorical variables.
~B. Segmented bar graph#Segmented bars compare categorical proportions.
~C. Scatterplot#A scatterplot needs two numerical variables.
=D. Dot plot#A dot plot can show the distribution of one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A two-way table shows club membership by grade level. What can be compared?
{
~A. The average value of club names#Club names are categories and cannot be averaged.
=B. Counts or proportions for category combinations#Two-way tables compare how two categorical variables are related.
~C. The slope of a numerical relationship#Slope belongs to numerical graphs, not two-way tables.
~D. The distribution of one measurement only#The table uses two categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which question would require bivariate data?
{
=A. Does screen time relate to hours of sleep?#This asks about two variables for each person.
~B. What is the most common pet type?#That uses one categorical variable.
~C. How many minutes do students read each day?#That uses one numerical variable.
~D. Which bus route is most common?#That uses one categorical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A scatterplot has points rising from left to right. What is a reasonable interpretation?
{
~A. One category has the highest count#Scatterplots do not show category counts.
~B. The variables are definitely causing each other#Association alone does not prove causation.
=C. Larger values of one numerical variable tend to go with larger values of the other#An upward pattern suggests a positive association.
~D. The data must be categorical#Scatterplots use numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which statement correctly distinguishes univariate from bivariate data?
{
~A. Univariate has one student; bivariate has two students#The number of cases is not the distinction.
=B. Univariate records one variable; bivariate records two variables for each case#The distinction is based on the number of variables.
~C. Univariate is categorical; bivariate is numerical#Either structure can include categorical or numerical variables.
~D. Univariate uses charts; bivariate uses tables#Display type does not define the structure.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram of test scores is used in a report. What question can it answer well?
{
=A. How test scores are distributed#A histogram shows the shape and frequency of one numerical variable.
~B. Whether study time relates to score#That would need a second variable.
~C. Which category students prefer#Test scores are numerical, not preference categories.
~D. Which student earned each exact score#A histogram groups values and may not identify individuals.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey records favorite sport and favorite music genre. What display could summarize the relationship?
{
~A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot requires numerical variables.
~B. Histogram#A histogram displays one numerical variable.
=C. Two-way table#Both variables are categorical, so a two-way table is appropriate.
~D. Dot plot#A dot plot displays one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records only shoe size. Which description is most accurate?
{
~A. Bivariate numerical#Only one variable is recorded.
=B. Univariate numerical#Shoe size is one numerical variable in this context.
~C. Univariate categorical#Shoe size is treated as a size measurement here.
~D. Bivariate categorical#There are not two categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Why must both number of variables and data type be considered when choosing a display?
{
=A. Because different displays fit different variable structures and data types#Display choice depends on whether data are categorical or numerical and one-variable or two-variable.
~B. Because all displays work for all data if labeled#Labels do not make an unsuitable display meaningful.
~C. Because only numerical data can be displayed#Categorical data can be displayed with bars, pie charts, and tables.
~D. Because data type matters but variable count never does#The number of variables determines whether a relationship display may be needed.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which situation is univariate numerical?
{
~A. Recording steps and calories burned#That records two numerical variables.
~B. Recording favorite exercise type#That records one categorical variable.
~C. Recording grade level and favorite sport#That records two categorical variables.
=D. Recording the number of steps each student walked yesterday#One numerical count is recorded for each student.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A dot plot and a histogram can both be used for what purpose?
{
~A. Showing a relationship between two numerical variables#A scatterplot is used for that purpose.
~B. Comparing two categorical variables#A two-way table or segmented bar graph fits that purpose.
=C. Showing the distribution of one numerical variable#Both displays summarize one numerical variable.
~D. Displaying only category labels#Dot plots and histograms are for numerical values.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A table records each student's age and whether the student rides the bus. What type of data set is this?
{
~A. Univariate numerical#There are two variables.
=B. Bivariate mixed data#Age is numerical, and bus-rider status is categorical.
~C. Bivariate numerical only#One variable is categorical.
~D. Univariate categorical#Age is an additional numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A two-way table shows counts but not percentages. What may need to be calculated to compare groups fairly?
{
=A. Relative frequencies#Relative frequencies help compare groups of different sizes.
~B. A line of best fit#Lines of best fit are used with scatterplots.
~C. The mean of category names#Category names cannot be averaged.
~D. The maximum numerical value#The table summarizes categories, not a numerical distribution.
}
